{"answer":"The proof’s parity argument is valid, but its stated conclusion is reversed. Assuming sqrt(2)=a/b with integers a,b in lowest terms gives a^2=2b^2. This forces a even; writing a=2c then forces b^2=2c^2 and hence b even. Thus 2 divides both a and b, contradicting that a/b is in lowest terms. The contradiction rules out the assumed rational representation, so sqrt(2) is irrational, not rational.","approaches":[{"description":"Assume a lowest-terms rational representation of sqrt(2), derive that both numerator and denominator are even, and discharge the assumption by contradiction.","id":"direct-reductio","outcome":"candidate","reason":"The parity implications are proved directly and the contradiction negates the assumed rationality."},{"description":"Treat the common evenness of a and b as evidence for rationality.","id":"reversed-conclusion","outcome":"rejected","reason":"Common evenness contradicts lowest terms; it cannot establish the assumed representation."}],"change_log":[],"claims":[{"basis":"question_premise","basis_reference":"Question premise: \"write sqrt(2)=a/b in lowest terms, square to get a^2=2b^2\"","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"lowest-terms-meaning","kind":"definition","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"For integers a and b, saying a/b is in lowest terms entails that a and b are not both divisible by 2.","step_ids":["define-lowest-terms"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"local_assumption","basis_reference":"Temporary assumption for reductio, using the representation described in the question.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":["contradict-lowest-terms"],"id":"rationality-assumption","kind":"assumption","scope_step_ids":["assume-representation","derive-squared-equation","prove-a-even","substitute-a-even","prove-b-even","contradict-lowest-terms"],"statement":"Assume temporarily that sqrt(2)=a/b for integers a,b with b nonzero and a/b in lowest terms.","step_ids":["assume-representation"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Algebraic squaring of the temporary representation.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["rationality-assumption"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"squared-equation","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Under the temporary assumption, a^2=2b^2.","step_ids":["derive-squared-equation"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Direct parity argument in prove-a-even.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["squared-equation"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"a-even","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"a is even.","step_ids":["prove-a-even"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Substitution of a=2c into a^2=2b^2.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["a-even","squared-equation"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"b-square-even","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"b^2 is even.","step_ids":["substitute-a-even"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"The same direct parity argument used for a, applied to b.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["b-square-even"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"b-even","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"b is even.","step_ids":["prove-b-even"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Both a and b are even, contrary to lowest terms.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["lowest-terms-meaning","a-even","b-even"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"lowest-terms-contradiction","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The temporary representation contradicts its lowest-terms condition.","step_ids":["contradict-lowest-terms"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Reductio discharging rationality-assumption at contradict-lowest-terms.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["lowest-terms-contradiction"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"sqrt-two-irrational","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":["contradict-lowest-terms"],"statement":"sqrt(2) is not rational.","step_ids":["contradict-lowest-terms"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"define-lowest-terms","justification":"If both integers were divisible by 2, then their fraction could be reduced by cancelling 2; thus a lowest-terms fraction cannot have both entries even.","statement":"Record the needed meaning of “in lowest terms”: a and b are not both even."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"assume-representation","justification":"This is the temporary negation of the desired irrationality conclusion.","statement":"Assume sqrt(2)=a/b for integers a,b, b nonzero, with a/b in lowest terms."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["assume-representation"],"id":"derive-squared-equation","justification":"Squaring gives 2=a^2/b^2; multiplying by b^2 gives a^2=2b^2.","statement":"Derive a^2=2b^2."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["derive-squared-equation"],"id":"prove-a-even","justification":"The right side is even. If a were odd, a=2k+1 for some integer k, so a^2=4k(k+1)+1, which is odd. This contradicts a^2=2b^2. Hence a is even.","statement":"Prove that a is even."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["derive-squared-equation","prove-a-even"],"id":"substitute-a-even","justification":"Write a=2c. Then 4c^2=2b^2, and division by 2 gives b^2=2c^2, so b^2 is even.","statement":"Show that b^2 is even."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["substitute-a-even"],"id":"prove-b-even","justification":"If b were odd, the calculation in prove-a-even, with b in place of a, would make b^2 odd, contrary to b^2=2c^2. Hence b is even.","statement":"Prove that b is even."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["define-lowest-terms","assume-representation","prove-a-even","prove-b-even"],"id":"contradict-lowest-terms","justification":"The assumption says a/b is in lowest terms, while prove-a-even and prove-b-even say both entries are divisible by 2. This contradiction discharges the temporary assumption.","statement":"Obtain a contradiction with lowest terms and conclude that no such rational representation exists."}],"question_status":"answered","tool_requests":[]}

